# Normalise to LF in the repository, check out native.
* text=auto

# cmd.exe is happiest with CRLF in a batch file.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Never let git touch these if any ever get committed.
*.wrpath binary
*.mtv binary
*.dll binary
*.exe binary
*.pdb binary

# Vendored third-party sources: exactly the bytes that came out of the upstream
# release archive, and NOT normalised.
#
# This is load-bearing rather than fussy. third_party\VERSION.txt records a
# SHA-256 for every file taken, so that a reviewer can hash their own copy and
# see it is unmodified. `* text=auto` above would store them LF-normalised and
# hand each cloner whatever their platform prefers -- and the two libraries do
# not even agree with each other about what came out of the archive: the LZMA
# SDK ships CRLF and miniz ships LF. Either way at least one platform's clone
# would hash differently from the record, which makes the record worse than
# useless: it would read as evidence of tampering on a clean checkout.
#
# -text rather than binary, so `git diff` against a future upstream bump is
# still readable.
third_party/** -text

# A recorded API conversation is bytes off somebody's server, not text of ours.
# See tests\api_tape.h.
tests/fixtures/api/** -text
